// This file contains brisks sourceTree, a sourceTree is a programmatic
// representation of your entire program including all modules,
// components and expressions. This is extracted directly from the parse
// tree and is used for everything in the compiler allowing us to resolve
// file paths and modules imports as quickly as possible.
module SourceTree

// The source tree itself is split up into two separate sets of trees
// At the top level we have a structural tree which contains all the
// modules and components in the program this defines the layout of the
// program itself, and linking dynamics.
// The second set of trees are the module bodies themselves which contains
// code that will actually run.
// Using this separation, we can think of it like this, the structural tree is
// used by the compiler to orchestrate the compilation process.
// The module bodies are used to represent the actual code that will be run by the
// program. This allows us to have a clear separation of concerns, makes
// linking dynamics easy to understand as they are syntactic in nature, and allows
// us to have a clear split between modules and components.
